756 0 obj
<<
/Title (Conventional planetary surface mapping techniques primarily rely on optical and imaging sensors, which are highly dependent on sunlight and are significantly affected by atmospheric conditions. These methods provide limited information about surface roughness and material properties and are ineffective for subsurface or all-weather observation. RADAR based techniques, particularly Synthetic Aperture RADAR \(SAR\), overcome these limitations by enabling high-resolution surface characterization independent of illumination and environmental conditions. However, existing RADAR and SAR systems used in planetary missions rely on highly specialized and costly instrumentation, which limits their practical implementation at smaller scales. This creates the need for a low-cost, flexible platform such as the Universal Software Radio Peripheral \(USRP\) to demonstrate radar-based planetary surface mapping concepts.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 618 0 R
>>
endobj
